# Banned Strings — Example Template
#
# Copy this file to `.banned-strings` (which is .gitignored) and fill in
# with any strings you want the pre-commit hook to block from public commits.
# Use one entry per line. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# Matching semantics:
#   - Fixed-string (literal) match, case-sensitive, with whole-word boundaries.
#   - Any character is supported in a banned string — no regex escaping needed.
#     You can safely list things like  C++  or  foo(bar  or  foo.bar  and they
#     are compared as literal text, not as regex patterns.
#   - Word boundaries mean the banned string only matches when surrounded by
#     non-word characters. "will" will NOT match inside "willpower".
#
# Example categories:
#
# Your project names:
# MyProject
# MyApp
#
# People and entities you don't want to mention:
# SomeCompany
# SomePerson
#
# Industry jargon from your work:
# DomainTerm
# JargonWord
#
# ALLOW LIST: strings that are banned elsewhere but OK in specific files.
# Format: "string | allowed_path1,allowed_path2"
#
# Paths in the allow-list are compared LITERALLY — exact string match against
# the file path as git reports it (relative to the repo root). No regex, no
# globs, no wildcards. If you want to allow the string in both README.md and
# LICENSE, list them both explicitly, separated by commas.
#
# Example:
# Your Name | LICENSE,README.md,docs/CHEATSHEET.md
